    | # land_vehicle.sh - land a reviewed vehicle bundle onto main (Helix Constitution).
    | #
    | # Repository path: scripts/release/land_vehicle.sh
    | # Test:            tests/release/test_land_vehicle.sh
    | #
    | # WHAT IT DOES
    | #   A vehicle is a git bundle holding commits that received an independent GO
    | #   but were not pushed. This script lands them on main of every upstream,
    | #   fast-forward only, in two phases so the lander can run the heavy derived-file
    | #   regeneration in between:
    | #
    | #   prepare  (1) verify the bundle sha256, `git bundle verify`, and that the
    | #                given head is a ref tip inside the bundle;
    | #            (2) create a fresh clone at <workdir>/<clone-name> (default name
    | #                `constitution`, required by the workable-items engine), fetch
    | #                main from every declared remote, refuse if the remote mains have
    | #                diverged, take the newest tip as main;
    | #            (3) mint the real tracker id by running --mint-cmd inside the clone
    | #                (the §11.4.202 BUG/TASK intake, e.g. a `--prefix CON` add); the
    | #                LAST stdout line must be the id (<KEY>-<digits>). The mint MUST
    | #                change at least one file in the clone (the tracker DB is tracked,
    | #                §11.4.95) and must not commit or switch branch by itself. Every
    | #                path it changes must be ADDED or MODIFIED (never deleted, renamed
    | #                or a symlink) and must match one of the LANDER_TRACKER_PATHS
    | #                patterns; the list (status and path) is written to
    | #                <evidence-dir>/mint_changes.tsv before it is checked. The id must
    | #                be NEW: it must not occur in the HEAD version of any modified
    | #                file, and it must occur, as a token, in the staged version of at
    | #                least one changed file. Otherwise prepare refuses and nothing is
    | #                landed. The changed files are committed as-is (never hand-edited)
    | #                as a commit ON MAIN, and that commit's name-status must equal
    | #                mint_changes.tsv. Alternatively --item-id gives an id that was
    | #                already minted. With --id-verify-cmd the id is then confirmed
    | #                through the engine's own read command (both paths);
    | #            (4) re-commit every unpushed vehicle commit whose Roadmap-Item
    | #                trailer is missing or is a placeholder (--placeholder-re), with the
    | #                real id, keeping author name, email, date and TREE. Only the
    | #                trailer block as git itself parses it (`git interpret-trailers
    | #                --parse`) is edited: a trailer-shaped line in body prose is left
    | #                alone, the key is matched case-insensitively, carriage returns are
    | #                stripped from the whole message, identical Roadmap-Item lines are
    | #                de-duplicated, and a missing trailer is appended with
    | #                `git interpret-trailers` (prepare aborts if that fails). Tree
    | #                equality per commit is checked with `git diff-tree` and written to
    | #                <evidence-dir>/tree_equality.tsv;
    | #            (5) integrate onto main, never rebasing and never rewriting main:
    | #                a fast-forward when main is an ancestor of the re-committed vehicle
    | #                (the --item-id path with no upstream movement), otherwise a merge
    | #                commit. On the --mint-cmd path the mint commit is already on main,
    | #                so the integration is ALWAYS a merge commit there. Then every
    | #                commit in main_before..main must carry exactly one Roadmap-Item
    | #                trailer (as git parses it) whose value is <KEY>-<digits>; then the
    | #                derived-file steps are PRINTED for the lander.
    | #   publish  (6) refuse a dirty tree, a clone not on main, a clone whose remote
    | #                URLs differ from --remotes-file, and a prepare state that does not
    | #                match the refs prepare recorded; refuse unless local main contains
    | #                both the prepared base and the landed vehicle head;
    | #            (7) preflight EVERY remote first (read the remote main, fetch it,
    | #                check it is an ancestor of local main). If any remote is
    | #                non-fast-forward, unreadable or has no main, NOTHING is pushed
    | #                (`not-pushed-preflight`) and publish exits 1. Then every commit in
    | #                `main --not <all fetched remote mains>` (the commits that are on
    | #                NO remote yet; independent of the state file) must carry one real
    | #                Roadmap-Item trailer, else nothing is pushed. A commit that is
    | #                already on at least one remote is not refused even when it reaches
    | #                another remote without a real trailer (refusing it could never
    | #                converge without a force push, and it was already published):
    | #                each such (remote, commit) is a WARN line and a row of
    | #                <evidence-dir>/publish_warnings.tsv and of the `## warnings`
    | #                section of publish.md; the exit code is not changed by a WARN.
    | #                Otherwise push `main:refs/heads/main` to each remote that is behind;
    | #            (8) `git ls-remote` read-back of every remote; a per-remote PASS/FAIL
    | #                table goes to <evidence-dir>/publish.md (+ .tsv); any FAIL => exit 1.
    | #   verify   step (8) alone (read-back only, no push). It needs the prepare state
    | #            and the same remote-URL check as publish.
    | #
    | # SAFETY
    | #   No force push of any kind: the only push is a plain `main:refs/heads/main`.
    | #   The test greps this file for force-type flags and refspecs prefixed with a plus
    | #   sign, and records the argv of every push the script makes through a git shim.
    | #   No remote URL is printed (URLs may carry credentials): git's own stderr from
    | #   fetch, ls-remote and push is not shown; it is appended to
    | #   <clone>/.git/land_git_stderr.log (that clone's .git/config holds the same URLs
    | #   already). Only remote names appear in output and evidence. Nothing is
    | #   deleted. HOME is not touched. --clone-name must be a single plain path
    | #   component, so the clone always stays inside the workdir; an existing path or a
    | #   (dangling) symlink at the clone path is refused. --workdir must not start with
    | #   '-' (so `cd` never reads it as an option), CDPATH is ignored. A bad line of the
    | #   remotes file is reported by its line number only, never by its text (the URL
    | #   may hold a credential).
    | #
    | # ENVIRONMENT (cleared before the first git call)
    | #   Variables that select or redirect a repository or inject config would make
    | #   every `git -C <clone>` act on another repository (git exports GIT_DIR and
    | #   GIT_INDEX_FILE to its hooks, so a lander started from a hook would write into
    | #   the caller's repository), and trace variables would print remote URLs. The
    | #   script unsets: GIT_DIR GIT_WORK_TREE GIT_INDEX_FILE GIT_OBJECT_DIRECTORY
    | #   GIT_ALTERNATE_OBJECT_DIRECTORIES GIT_COMMON_DIR GIT_CONFIG GIT_CONFIG_PARAMETERS
    | #   GIT_CONFIG_COUNT GIT_CONFIG_KEY_* GIT_CONFIG_VALUE_* GIT_IMPLICIT_WORK_TREE
    | #   GIT_GRAFT_FILE GIT_NO_REPLACE_OBJECTS GIT_REPLACE_REF_BASE GIT_PREFIX
    | #   GIT_SHALLOW_FILE GIT_NAMESPACE GIT_QUARANTINE_PATH GIT_PUSH_OPTION_* GIT_TRACE*
    | #   GIT_CURL_VERBOSE GIT_EXTERNAL_DIFF GIT_CEILING_DIRECTORIES
    | #   GIT_DISCOVERY_ACROSS_FILESYSTEM, plus every name `git rev-parse --local-env-vars`
    | #   prints, and CDPATH. It keeps GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL, GIT_CONFIG_SYSTEM,
    | #   GIT_CONFIG_NOSYSTEM (the caller's choice of config FILES), the ssh/askpass
    | #   variables and the author/committer identity. The mint and id-verify commands
    | #   inherit the cleared environment.
    | #   LANDER_REMOTES   names of the remotes the remotes file must declare (each
    | #                    ^[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._-]*$), default the 9 upstreams.
    | #   LANDER_TRACKER_PATHS  space-separated bash patterns (`*` also matches '/') of
    | #                    the paths the mint may add or modify, relative to the clone
    | #                    root, default `docs/workable_items.db`. Its -wal/-shm sidecars
    | #                    are gitignored: a mint that changes only them changes no
    | #                    tracked file and is refused (fail closed).
    | #
    | # REAL ENGINE (mint command)
    | #   The workable-items engine prints `add: created CON-166 (Task, status=Queued) in
    | #   Issues`, not a bare id, so wrap it to print the id as the last line, e.g.
    | #     --mint-cmd "<engine> add Task low --db docs/workable_items.db --title '...' \
    | #       --prefix CON | sed -n 's/^add: created \(CON-[0-9][0-9]*\) .*/\1/p'"
    | #   (under `bash -c` without pipefail the sed decides the status; an engine
    | #   failure leaves the last line empty, so prepare refuses it as "did not print an
    | #   id"). Try such a command first on a COPY of the database, never on the live
    | #   checkout.
    | #
    | # LOCK
    | #   Every subcommand takes an exclusive `flock` on <workdir>/.land_vehicle.lock
    | #   for its whole run; a second concurrent run in the same workdir is refused
    | #   (exit 2). The lock is released by the kernel when the process ends, so there
    | #   is no stale lock to clean up. Every git command, the mint and the id-verify
    | #   commands run without the lock descriptor (`9>&-`), so a process they leave
    | #   behind (a gpg agent, a credential helper) never keeps the lock.
    | #
    | # HOOKS, CONFIG AND SIGNATURES (policy)
    | #   Every git command the script runs against the clone (init, fetch, checkout,
    | #   merge, commit, push, ls-remote) runs with `-c core.hooksPath=/dev/null` and
    | #   `-c core.fsmonitor=false`, and commit, merge and push also pass `--no-verify`,
    | #   so ambient hooks (pre-push, post-merge, post-checkout, reference-transaction,
    | #   commit-msg ...) never run. The push also runs with `-c push.followTags=false
    | #   -c push.recurseSubmodules=no --no-follow-tags`, so an ambient push.followTags
    | #   never pushes a tag, and publish checks the tree with `status --porcelain
    | #   --untracked-files=all`, so status.showUntrackedFiles=no never hides a file.
    | #   Other ambient config (for example a clean/smudge filter, push.pushOption) is
    | #   NOT neutralised; a url.insteadOf rewrite in a config FILE makes the remote-URL
    | #   check refuse. Commit signing follows the caller's git config. The mint and
    | #   id-verify commands run in the caller's environment. Re-committed vehicle
    | #   commits are made with `git commit-tree`, which runs no hooks and DROPS any
    | #   gpgsig signature of the original; tree_equality.tsv records this per commit
    | #   (column signature_dropped). The GO binds through tree equality, not through
    | #   the commit id or signature.
    | #
    | # TRAILER SHAPES REFUSED ON PURPOSE (fail closed)
    | #   A message whose last paragraph is a `#` comment line after the trailer block,
    | #   and a folded (continued) Roadmap-Item value, are NOT repaired: the result
    | #   would not hold exactly one parseable trailer, so prepare refuses it in its
    | #   post-check. Fix such a commit by hand in the vehicle and re-review it.
    | #   `<KEY>-050` is a placeholder by default: a real item numbered 050 would be
    | #   rewritten to the minted id. Pass --placeholder-re to change that.
    | #
    | # RE-RUN / RECOVERY
    | #   prepare refuses an existing clone path. prepare pushes nothing, so after a
    | #   failed prepare remove <workdir>/<clone-name> with a guarded delete and run
    | #   prepare again. publish is safe to re-run: remotes already at local main are
    | #   `up-to-date`, the rest are pushed (after the all-remote preflight).
    | #   An empty (new) mirror without a main branch makes prepare stop with "fetch of
    | #   main failed"; seed that mirror by hand first (fast-forward from another).
    | #
    | # USAGE
    | #   land_vehicle.sh prepare --bundle FILE --sha256 HEX --head SHA --workdir DIR \
    | #       --remotes-file FILE (--mint-cmd CMD | --item-id <KEY>-N) [--id-verify-cmd CMD] \
    | #       [--evidence-dir DIR] [--placeholder-re ERE] [--clone-name NAME] [--key-prefix KEY]
    | #   land_vehicle.sh publish --workdir DIR --remotes-file FILE [--evidence-dir DIR] \
    | #       [--key-prefix KEY] [--clone-name NAME]
    | #   land_vehicle.sh verify  --workdir DIR --remotes-file FILE [--evidence-dir DIR] [--clone-name NAME]
    | #   land_vehicle.sh -h | --help
    | #
    | #   --key-prefix: tracker id key (§11.4.54: the key is data), default CON. The id
    | #   must match ^<KEY>-[0-9]+$. publish reads the key from the prepare state; a
    | #   different --key-prefix given to publish is refused.
    | #   --placeholder-re default: ^(<KEY>-050|<KEY>-PENDING-[A-Za-z0-9._-]*|<TO-BE-MINTED[^>]*>)$
    | #   --id-verify-cmd: run in the clone as `bash -c CMD land_vehicle ID` (the id is $1
    | #   and $LAND_ITEM_ID); it must exit 0 and print the id as a token.
    | #   --clone-name: ^[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._-]*$ without '..'.
    | #   --remotes-file: one "name url" per line (# comments allowed). It must declare
    | #   exactly the names in LANDER_REMOTES (default: the 9 constitution upstreams),
    | #   and publish/verify refuse unless the clone's fetch URL and its only push URL
    | #   for every name equal the URL in the file.
    | #   Build it from the real checkout with: git remote -v | awk '$3=="(push)"{print $1, $2}'
    | #
    | # EXIT CODES
    | #   0 success | 1 verification or publish FAIL | 2 usage/config error (incl. lock
    | #   held, remote URL mismatch, bad state) | 3 integration refused (diverged mains,
    | #   merge conflict, nothing to land). Any other status of a failing command is
    | #   reported and mapped to 1.
